{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,280p' /app/agent-output/S14-link-install-lock-trap/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"# S14 — Link-install file-lock trap\n\n## Finding\n\nThis is a linked installation, not a copied dependency. The profile entry\n`C:\\Users\\me\\.dsh\\profiles\\web\\node_modules\\@org\\dsh-attach-input` is a\nWindows directory Junction:\n\n```text\nLinkType : Junction\nTarget   : {E:\\dev\\dsh-attach-input}\n```\n\nThat is recorded in `profile-introspection.txt:3-5`. The source path itself has\nno link target (`profile-introspection.txt:9-11`), and the profile patch also\nrecords `# link:E:\\dev\\dsh-attach-input` (`profile-introspection.txt:13-14`).\n\nA Junction makes the profile path and the repo path two names for the same\ndirectory tree. Consequently, the repo's `lib/client.js` and `lib/index.js`\nwere already the files visible to the profile. There was never a separate\nprofile copy to deploy to. Editing the repo was the deployment step; the\nrunning host still had to be restarted to load the edits.\n\n## The locks and the stale browser result\n\nThere are two relevant loaded files, `lib/client.js` and `lib/index.js`, and\nthe owner of both file handles in this incident is the running dsh web host,\nnot the browser:\n\n- The host loads `index.js` as the server-side plugin entry and keeps the\n  plugin/runtime files in use.\n- The same running host serves the browser-side `client.js` bundle. A browser\n  tab refresh does not restart that host or make it reload the already loaded\n  plugin, so it can continue serving the old client bundle.\n\nThe first copy reports that `client.js` is being used by another process\n(`copy-session.txt:1-4`). Retrying after closing the browser still reports the\nsame `EBUSY`, now for `client.js`, `index.js`, and `package.json`\n(`copy-session.txt:6-8`). Closing a tab cannot release handles owned by the\nstill-running dsh host. The browser can also retain a cached response, but it\nis not the process holding these source files open in this case.\n\n## Why the rename removed the source entries\n\n`$dst` was the `lib` directory reached through the Junction\n(`copy-session.txt:11`). Therefore these commands:\n\n```powershell\nRename-Item ($dst + '\\client.js') 'client.js.old2'\nRename-Item ($dst + '\\index.js') 'index.js.old2'\n```\n\nrenamed the actual files in `E:\\dev\\dsh-attach-input\\lib`, not files in an\nindependent profile directory. The subsequent copy used the source path, but\n`lib\\client.js` had just been renamed away, so it correctly failed with\n“source does not exist” (`copy-session.txt:12-15`). Both path spellings then\nshowed only `client.js.old2` and `index.js.old2`\n(`copy-session.txt:16-22`).\n\n### Exact recovery from the current state\n\nFirst fully stop the dsh web host and wait for it to exit; this releases its\nhandles before any recovery operation. Do not copy from the repo to the\nprofile path. With the evidence-confirmed current state (the two `.old2`\nfiles exist and the original names do not), restore the names in the repo:\n\n```powershell\n$repo = 'E:\\dev\\dsh-attach-input'\n$lib  = Join-Path $repo 'lib'\n\nRename-Item (Join-Path $lib 'client.js.old2') 'client.js'\nRename-Item (Join-Path $lib 'index.js.old2')  'index.js'\n\nGet-ChildItem $lib client.js,index.js\nnode --check (Join-Path $lib 'client.js')\nnode --check (Join-Path $lib 'index.js')\n```\n\nThe `.old2` files are the original edited entry files, so restoring their\nnames recovers the hover-preview and host-route edits. The profile Junction\nwill show the restored names automatically.\n\n## Ordered activation procedure\n\nAfter the syntax checks pass:\n\n1. Fully stop the dsh web host, including any child/worker process that loads\n   the plugin. A browser-tab close is not a substitute for this and is not\n   needed to deploy a Junction.\n2. Restore `client.js` and `index.js` as above while the host is stopped. Do\n   not run an install or `Copy-Item` into `node_modules`.\n3. Run `node --check` on both shipped files. Confirm the two original entry\n   names exist and the `.old2` names are no longer the active names.\n4. Start/restart the dsh web host and wait until the web service is ready.\n   This makes the host load the new `index.js`, register the new read-only\n   route, and serve the new `client.js`.\n5. In the browser, perform a hard refresh (`Ctrl+Shift+R` or `Ctrl+F5`). A\n   normal refresh can reuse a cached `client.js` response even after the host\n   has restarted; the hard refresh requests the new client bundle. If needed,\n   keep DevTools Network open with “Disable cache” enabled for the reload.\n6. Verify the host-side and browser-side changes independently: exercise the\n   new host route and check the host startup/plugin log, then use DevTools’\n   Network response (or Sources) for `client.js` to confirm the response\n   contains the hover-preview code and that the image-attachment preview\n   appears. This proves the new files were loaded rather than merely that the\n   tab rendered.\n\nThe host must be stopped and restarted because it owns the loaded plugin and\nthe file handles. The browser does not need a full process restart; it needs\na cache-bypassing reload after the host is serving the new bundle.\n\n## Pre-flight install-mode check\n\nBefore touching any file, inspect the profile entry and its patch marker:\n\n```powershell\n$entry = 'C:\\Users\\me\\.dsh\\profiles\\web\\node_modules\\@org\\dsh-attach-input'\nGet-Item $entry | Select-Object FullName,LinkType,Target\nGet-Content 'C:\\Users\\me\\.dsh\\profiles\\web\\cordis.patch.yml' |\n  Select-String 'dsh-attach-input'\n```\n\nInterpret the result as follows:\n\n- `LinkType : Junction` plus a `Target` pointing to the working tree, and a\n  `# link:<repo>` marker in `cordis.patch.yml`, means link install. Edit the\n  target repo and restart the host; do not copy into the profile entry.\n- A normal directory with blank `LinkType`/empty `Target`, and no link marker,\n  means a copied dependency. Only that mode has a distinct profile tree that\n  might require a deliberate copy/install update.\n\nGeneric “copy the files into `node_modules`” advice is actively harmful for a\nJunction: source and destination resolve to the same directory. With the host\nrunning, the self-copy encounters its open handles and returns `EBUSY`; a\nrename-aside workaround mutates the repo itself and can leave the shared\ninstallation with no entry files.\n\nEvidence cited: `/app/fixture/profile-introspection.txt`,\n`/app/fixture/copy-session.txt`, and `/app/fixture/maintainer-thread.md`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
